event('221-6(b)','targeted proof check','For the local affine-line model, a finite flat degree-n divisor is cut out by a unique monic polynomial of degree n. Its coefficients are the elementary symmetric functions, which also give coordinates on the symmetric power; the determinant norm has exactly these coefficients. Thus these two local maps are inverse on families, not merely on geometric points. Smooth curves reduce étale locally around each finite support to this model, and the constructions agree on overlaps. This checks the relative smooth-curve assertion under the standing hypotheses of (a).','221, p. 275, (b)')
event('221-6-Cartier-cycle','targeted proof check','On a regular scheme, an effective Cartier divisor is determined by its orders at codimension-one points: locally the regular local ring is factorial, and equal orders imply local equations differ by a unit. The parenthetic possible equality with a Chow variety is explicitly tentative and is not proved by this pointwise argument.','221, p. 275, paragraph after (b)')
event('221-7-Serre-Nagata-example','carried as background without fault found','The existence of the cited proper smooth nonprojective threefold and quadratic descent obstruction is read as a cited example; its construction is not reproduced here. No separability hypothesis is inserted into the existential printed sentence. The correction X/S prime only restores the domain of the already specified Weil restriction.','221, p. 275, section 7 opening')
event('221-7-Hilbert-nonexistence-implication','targeted proof check','Conditional on the cited nonrepresentable restriction example with separable quadratic S prime/S, its section functor is the open locus of degree-two étale subschemes of X whose map to S prime is an isomorphism. After the quadratic base change this is the locus with one point on each of the two conjugate components, and descent identifies it with the restriction functor. Representability of Hilb^2, or of its étale locus, would therefore represent the restriction. The distinct-point open in a symmetric square would give the same contradiction by (a). The underlying example itself remains background.','221, p. 275, Cela implique paragraph')
event('221-7-proper-field-section-scheme','carried as background without fault found','The proper-over-a-field extension using Chow domination and the cited finite-morphism factorization is retained as the author states it without proof. The stated automatic flatness of the dominating scheme over the field is verified, since every module over a field is flat; the descent/factorization argument is not independently reconstructed here.','221, p. 275, Par contre paragraph')
event('221-7-general-base-question','carried as background without fault found','The general-base assertion is posed as a question, not asserted as a theorem. The closed-subscheme special case is introduced here and continues on the next page; no result is inferred from the unfinished sentence.','221, p. 275, final lines')
